{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"S1 T1 PHASE 4e VALIDATION against the held-out oracle. Restored all 27 tests: 25 passed, 2 failed. BOTH failures diagnosed as TEST DEFECTS, not implementation faults, and both were verified empirically rather than taken on the implementer's word. (a) test_save_persists_rotation_before_replacing_live returned ['live','live']: proved by probe that get_rotation.os IS binding_repository.os IS the stdlib os module, so my two monkeypatch.setattr calls collided and the second won. Fixed to discriminate by DESTINATION path under a single patch. (b) test_corrupt_retired_alert_is_deduped_within_the_window expected 1 alert, got 2: alert_store.is_deduped compares now - rec.get('timestamp_ns', 0) against a 24h window, and neither BindingStore._alert nor alert_store.append ever stamps timestamp_ns, so the suppression can never fire. A DIFFERENTIAL PROBE constructing both BindingStore (the characterization source) and BindingRepository over the same corrupt file twice returned 2 alerts with no timestamp_ns for BOTH. The implementation is therefore FAITHFUL and my test was over-specified against an aspirational docstring; asserting 1 would have forced a behavior change this pure-extraction slice is explicitly forbidden from making. Test rewritten to pin real behavior with the reasoning recorded in its docstring; the unstamped-timestamp dedup gap is a genuine latent defect and goes to the Phase-5 residual-gap sweep as FILED, not accepted. After both fixes: 27/27 GREEN. TEETH: 5 defect-seeded mutations each went RED - reversed rotation/live ordering, scrubbing the inline floor despite a failed-open sidecar, live corruption failing open instead of closed, dropped temp-file cleanup, and handing out a copy instead of an open view. REFACTORING LITMUS: a behavior-preserving rename of a private helper, a private attribute and a constant left all 27 green, so these are not change-detectors. PHASE 4f PRE-FLIGHT: make lint exit 0 (after I fixed 3 RUF043 unescaped-dot regexes and one formatter reflow in my own test file - trivial lint only), make typecheck Success 561 files, 2808 tests pass across tests/unit/rebar_reconciler plus the module-size contract, scripts/check_config_reads.py 0 findings. Sizes: new binding_repository.py 360 LOC (inside the 200-500 target), binding_store.py untouched at 799, both under the strictly-greater-than 800 cap. Implementer reported 1 attempt to green and added one additive accessor, retired_locals(), which T2 needs for retired_key_for_local. COORDINATOR: operator settled the RP-04 S7.3.a coupling as advisory (soft relates_to, no depends_on, S7.3.a not blocked), RP-02 continues unpaused, and the three code-level guardrails are now binding on my implementer briefs - S2 preserves _env_int ambient sourcing byte-identically and records the move in ADR 0099; S3 T3 keeps its diff tight to _observe_rich_reemit and its docstring without touching adjacent _rich_cutover_active; S3 T2 limits run_differs.py to the invocation with the classifier in binding_recovery.py. New modules stay free of load_config(). The RP-04 retarget note will be FILED discovered_from brainy-cooked-hog in the Phase-5 sweep."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787096713537499001,"uuid":"0ed1d367-9566-47b5-ab6c-7dc4dad662a5"}